  "text": "In July I got hired for a remote personal-assistant job I found on a job board. My 'manager' at Brightline Logistics mailed me a check for $2,450 to buy home-office equipment from their approved vendor. I deposited it in this app on Aug 3. On Aug 6 the app showed the whole amount as available, so I figured the check had cleared, and my manager told me to Zelle $1,900 to OfficePro Supply for a laptop and printer and keep the rest as my first week's pay. I sent it that afternoon. The equipment never showed up and the manager stopped answering. Then on Aug 11 Summit Valley took the whole $2,450 back, charged me a $15 fee, and now I'm more than $2,000 negative. Nobody at the credit union ever warned me the check could be fake. The app said the money was available, which to me means it cleared. I'm asking Summit Valley to wipe out the negative balance and the fee. They made the money available, so they should eat the loss, not a college student. I already reported Brightline to the job board. 0 stars if I could."
